  "applicability_scope": [
    "User-reported Level 1 review of P14 Overview Theorems 1 and 2, plus every definition required to interpret their hypotheses and conclusions.",
    "Theorem 1: presentable indexed families are k-list identifiable exactly when they satisfy the recursive k-Angluin condition.",
    "Theorem 2: k-list identifiability is equivalent to a cover by exactly k index subcollections with relative Angluin tell-tales.",
    "Shared indexed languages, streams, exact positive presentations (repetitions permitted), finite histories, fixed-width lists of indices, extensional target inclusion, and eventual list correctness.",
    "Psi, finite tell-tales, strict sublanguages, recursive depth and its zero convention, KAngluinCondition, relative tell-tales, relative Condition Two, index covers, and HasAngluinStratification.",
    "The explicit presentability hypothesis, classical semantic model, zero-width totalization, duplicate indices, and possibly overlapping/empty/repeated strata remain in force. The source permits finite as well as infinite nonempty languages."
  ],
